WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.383
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:09.000
It's Sunday morning, I think.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.420
My room is dark.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.300
My head is the throbbing.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.122
I have a knot in my stomach,
nausea mixed with anxiety.

00:00:16.122 --> 00:00:20.845
[SOUND] Why did I take all
those tequila shots last night?

00:00:20.845 --> 00:00:24.370
Why do I feel like I need to
apologize to my best friend again?

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:30.280
I promised myself, just like I did
last Sunday, that going forward,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.040
things will be different.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.990
No more shots, I am done feeling
like this when I wake up.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.810
Week after week I made these
Sunday morning promises to myself,

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.210
only to break them by Friday night.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.600
I just couldn't let go of alcohol.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.575
It played a huge role in my life,
both socially and professionally.

00:00:50.575 --> 00:00:55.958
I was afraid that if I quit drinking I
would feel different from everyone else,

00:00:55.958 --> 00:00:58.987
or that I won't be seen
as part of the group.

00:00:58.987 --> 00:01:01.222
Yet, I knew i could make a change.

00:01:01.222 --> 00:01:04.600
After years of struggling to
keep my Sunday morning promises,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.606
I finally sought support to
quit drinking last summer.

00:01:09.358 --> 00:01:11.677
And trust me,
that was a change for the better.

00:01:13.315 --> 00:01:16.620
I'm sure many of you
may relate to my story.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.740
It's not that dramatic,
just a bunch of ugly Sunday mornings.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.150
But even so,
I found quitting drinking really hard.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.970
In the early days, I couldn't go to bars
or be around people drinking alcohol.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.464
And I'd say I still had an easier
time than a lot of people.

00:01:33.464 --> 00:01:35.374
For those with more ingrained addiction,

00:01:35.374 --> 00:01:37.450
it can be nearly impossible
to quit drinking.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.938
And if you are able to quit,

00:01:39.938 --> 00:01:42.912
you face a world that too
often revolves around alcohol.

00:01:44.758 --> 00:01:48.779
Getting sober has helped me understand
that keeping alcohol and such a prevalent

00:01:48.779 --> 00:01:52.220
role in society disadvantages and
excludes those who don't drink.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.290
Whether people abstain due to pregnancy,
religious beliefs, recovery, or

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.240
personal preference,
we are creating a divide

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.430
that keeps many non-drinkers out of
our most important conversations.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.580
This exclusion, whether intentional or
not, means we're missing out on

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.470
the valuable untapped perspectives
of many of these people.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.558
And we are not talking about
a small group of people here.

00:02:18.558 --> 00:02:22.660
In fact, Alcohol.org, a subsidiary
of American Addiction Centers,

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.660
estimates that 30% of US adults
don't consume any alcohol.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.070
And this number continues to rise
as millennials become more and

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:31.960
more health conscious.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.445
There's even a new trend taking hold
called the sober curious movement.

00:02:36.445 --> 00:02:39.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] With the non-drinking

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.308
portion of the population on the rise,
now is the time for

00:02:42.308 --> 00:02:46.519
us to figure out how to include everyone
in our business and social interactions.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.200
The first thing we can
do is change our events.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.860
Many networking events
today take place at bars.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.660
Given my history, a bar with unlimited
alcohol is not an appealing place for

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:00.390
me to meet people.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.760
For instance, earlier this year,
I was at a GSP networking event.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.247
I was walking around in
circles without a drink,

00:03:09.247 --> 00:03:12.720
desperately trying to find the courage
to join a group to chat with.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.230
And let me tell you, going up to groups
of strangers at a 400-person event

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.855
with no social lubricant is really hard.

00:03:20.855 --> 00:03:23.874
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Every time I tried to join in with

00:03:23.874 --> 00:03:28.993
someone new, they would try to hand me a
drink or ask about why I wasn't drinking.

00:03:28.993 --> 00:03:32.073
And for the people I was talking to,
well, at the end of the night,

00:03:32.073 --> 00:03:33.975
a lot of them weren't making any sense.

00:03:33.975 --> 00:03:39.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For me, the experience was miserable.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.390
I dreaded going to future events,
but knew I had to.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.581
Or risk missing out on the connections and

00:03:44.581 --> 00:03:47.720
relationships that stem from
many of these interactions.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.589
And in these situations,
I'm one of the lucky ones.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.700
I can still tolerate going to these
events, even if I'd prefer not to.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.891
My voice is still heard.

00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:05.247
But many non-drinkers aren't so lucky,
and will miss out on key opportunities

00:04:05.247 --> 00:04:10.170
by staying away from alcohol-focused
events in the name of self-care.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.740
We can start to level the playing field
for non-drinkers by thinking about

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.850
alternative, alcohol-free ways for
us to meet and interact with one another.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:23.185
Planning a hike or a cooking class as a
team event in lieu of a happy hour is all

00:04:23.185 --> 00:04:25.653
it takes to start increasing inclusivity.

00:04:28.055 --> 00:04:32.350
The next thing we can do is think
about the alternatives we offer.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:36.232
It should be just as easy to find a soda
as it is to find a gin and tonic.

00:04:36.232 --> 00:04:41.205
Keeping non-drinkers limited to just water
or whatever mixers are on hand while

00:04:41.205 --> 00:04:45.372
everyone else gets to choose from
a plethora of alcoholic options,

00:04:45.372 --> 00:04:48.901
sends a signal that non-drinkers
are an afterthought.

00:04:48.901 --> 00:04:50.430
Or worse, weren't thought of at all.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.700
And I don't know about you, but
when I feel like an afterthought time and

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.780
time again, I stop showing up.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.720
If we don't show non-drinkers
that they're welcome,

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:02.899
they'll stop coming to our events.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.390
Luckily, coming up with alternatives is
a really easy way to have a big impact.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:15.877
Earlier this year, all of us MBA I's
participated in the Executive Challenge.

00:05:15.877 --> 00:05:19.845
It was a full-day competition that
culminated in a squad celebration and

00:05:19.845 --> 00:05:21.510
a class-wide cocktail hour.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.580
As I walked to the room where my squad
would be having our post competition pow

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.050
wow, I felt dread take over me as I heard

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:33.280
pops of champagne coming from
every single room around me.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.890
Great, I thought, another time where
I'll be the only person not drinking.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.961
I begrudgingly walked to my team room,
only to see my squad fellow sitting there

00:05:43.961 --> 00:05:47.232
with a huge smile and
a bottle of sparkling grape juice.

00:05:47.232 --> 00:05:51.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My mood immediately changed.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:53.970
I didn't feel like an afterthought or
an outsider.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.815
What was a small gesture from him
completely changed the way I saw my role

00:05:58.815 --> 00:05:59.512
in the group.

00:06:01.451 --> 00:06:02.574
Changing our events and

00:06:02.574 --> 00:06:06.760
our alternatives are just two ways
that we can start to have an impact.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:10.397
But we can have an even bigger impact by
changing our dialogue around alcohol.

00:06:12.604 --> 00:06:16.930
About a month after I got sober, I met
some friends for brunch in San Francisco.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.700
We sat down at our table and a waiter
came over to walk us through the menu.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:26.710
When he got to the drink section,
he joked,

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.800
if you're here to have a good time,
here the cocktails.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:31.980
If you're no fun, here the mocktails.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.830
A month earlier,
I would have thought the joke was cute.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:39.900
But in that moment,
it almost reduced me to tears.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:44.330
I was upset about having to quit drinking.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:47.220
I was feeling really different from my
friends, who could all drink normally.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:54.027
I felt alone due to my inability to
participate in a favorite group pastime.

00:06:54.027 --> 00:06:56.410
I didn't even know who I
was as a sober person.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:01.720
In just one sentence, that waiter managed
to put my worst fears into words.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.438
Now that I wasn't drinking,
I feared I'd be no fun.

00:07:07.214 --> 00:07:11.780
Later that same day, a friend at
dinner started to make a toast.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.325
I raised my glass of water,
hoping to go unnoticed.

00:07:14.325 --> 00:07:17.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; However, I was quickly chastised.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.225
Cheersing with water is bad luck.

00:07:20.225 --> 00:07:27.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I've thought a lot about why I drank.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:31.606
About why it was so hard for
me to keep my Sunday morning promises.

00:07:31.606 --> 00:07:34.950
For a while, alcohol was fun.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.639
It helped me confident,
when inside I was self-conscious.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.508
But most importantly,
it helped me feel like part of the group.

00:07:43.412 --> 00:07:46.504
But all those illusions started to
fall away as I lost control over my

00:07:46.504 --> 00:07:47.870
relationship with alcohol.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:51.533
For me, drinking is no longer fun.

00:07:53.173 --> 00:07:55.981
We need to change the narrative
around what is normal and fun,

00:07:55.981 --> 00:07:57.339
when it comes to alcohol use.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.260
Making someone feel abnormal, or

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.700
like they're no fun because
they're not drinking, is not okay.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:08.880
A good rule of thumb,
keep your comments about drinking and

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.335
not drinking to yourself.

00:08:10.335 --> 00:08:11.969
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:08:11.969 --> 00:08:15.860
if you hear someone making a comment that
might make a non-drinker uncomfortable,

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:18.452
I encourage you to be the one
to step in and make it right.

00:08:20.437 --> 00:08:24.140
We here at the GSB have a special
responsibility to make a change.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:28.760
As future leaders, society will
look to each of us as role models.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:34.120
We can start to make a change today by
changing our events, our alternatives, and

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:35.408
our dialogue around alcohol.

00:08:35.408 --> 00:08:39.365
So with that, I'd like to raise a toast.

00:08:39.365 --> 00:08:42.615
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; With water, because it is not bad luck.

00:08:42.615 --> 00:08:44.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; To each of us here today.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:50.179
Each of us has the power to leave
this room and make a difference,

00:08:50.179 --> 00:08:52.287
both here at the GSB and beyond.

00:08:52.287 --> 00:08:54.266
So cheers to changing the future.

00:08:54.266 --> 00:08:58.276
To creating environments where both
drinkers and non-drinkers can thrive,

00:08:58.276 --> 00:08:58.890
thank you.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.697
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:00.697 --> 00:09:05.575
[MUSIC]

